#doc(title: [Mechanics: Test 3], subject: "WPH11 practice paper")

= Instructions

Answer *all* questions. Write your answers in the spaces provided. The total mark for this paper is 40.

- Use a black pen.
- Show your working in every calculation.
- Give numerical answers to an appropriate number of significant figures.

= Section A

+ A ball is thrown vertically upwards at 12 m/s from the edge of a cliff 30 m high. Calculate the time it takes to reach the sea below, and its speed just before it does.
+ A trolley of mass 2.0 kg is pushed along a smooth horizontal track by a constant force. Its speed doubles from 1.5 m/s to 3.0 m/s in 4.0 s. Find the force on it and the work done.
+ State Newton's third law of motion, and explain why the forces in a pair never cancel each other.
+ A car of mass 1200 kg rounds a bend of radius 50 m at a constant speed of 15 m/s. Calculate the resultant force on the car and state its direction.

= Section B

A projectile is launched at 20 degrees to the horizontal with a speed of 10 m/s. Ignoring air resistance, determine its range, its maximum height and its time of flight. Comment on how the answers would change if air resistance were taken into account.

A projectile is launched at 25 degrees to the horizontal with a speed of 11 m/s. Ignoring air resistance, determine its range, its maximum height and its time of flight. Comment on how the answers would change if air resistance were taken into account.

A projectile is launched at 30 degrees to the horizontal with a speed of 12 m/s. Ignoring air resistance, determine its range, its maximum height and its time of flight. Comment on how the answers would change if air resistance were taken into account.

A projectile is launched at 35 degrees to the horizontal with a speed of 13 m/s. Ignoring air resistance, determine its range, its maximum height and its time of flight. Comment on how the answers would change if air resistance were taken into account.

A projectile is launched at 40 degrees to the horizontal with a speed of 14 m/s. Ignoring air resistance, determine its range, its maximum height and its time of flight. Comment on how the answers would change if air resistance were taken into account.

A projectile is launched at 45 degrees to the horizontal with a speed of 15 m/s. Ignoring air resistance, determine its range, its maximum height and its time of flight. Comment on how the answers would change if air resistance were taken into account.

A uniform ladder of length 4.0 m and weight 210 N rests against a smooth vertical wall, with its foot on rough horizontal ground. Draw a free-body diagram of the ladder and find the least coefficient of friction that stops it from slipping.

A uniform ladder of length 5.0 m and weight 220 N rests against a smooth vertical wall, with its foot on rough horizontal ground. Draw a free-body diagram of the ladder and find the least coefficient of friction that stops it from slipping.

A uniform ladder of length 6.0 m and weight 230 N rests against a smooth vertical wall, with its foot on rough horizontal ground. Draw a free-body diagram of the ladder and find the least coefficient of friction that stops it from slipping.

A uniform ladder of length 4.0 m and weight 240 N rests against a smooth vertical wall, with its foot on rough horizontal ground. Draw a free-body diagram of the ladder and find the least coefficient of friction that stops it from slipping.

A uniform ladder of length 5.0 m and weight 250 N rests against a smooth vertical wall, with its foot on rough horizontal ground. Draw a free-body diagram of the ladder and find the least coefficient of friction that stops it from slipping.

A uniform ladder of length 6.0 m and weight 260 N rests against a smooth vertical wall, with its foot on rough horizontal ground. Draw a free-body diagram of the ladder and find the least coefficient of friction that stops it from slipping.

A uniform ladder of length 4.0 m and weight 270 N rests against a smooth vertical wall, with its foot on rough horizontal ground. Draw a free-body diagram of the ladder and find the least coefficient of friction that stops it from slipping.

A uniform ladder of length 5.0 m and weight 280 N rests against a smooth vertical wall, with its foot on rough horizontal ground. Draw a free-body diagram of the ladder and find the least coefficient of friction that stops it from slipping.

#if variant == "markscheme" [
  #let g = 9.81

  = Mark scheme

  + Time to reach the sea: #calc.round((12 + calc.sqrt(12 * 12 + 2 * g * 30)) / g, digits: 2) s; speed: #calc.round(calc.sqrt(12 * 12 + 2 * g * 30), digits: 1) m/s. Award one mark for each, and one for using g = 9.81 m/s².
  + Force: 0.75 N; work done: 6.75 J.
  + Award one mark for the statement and one for noting that the forces act on different bodies.
  + Resultant force: 5400 N, towards the centre of the bend.

  Section B, first projectile: range #calc.round(10 * 10 * calc.sin(40deg) / g, digits: 2) m, maximum height #calc.round(calc.pow(10 * calc.sin(20deg), 2) / (2 * g), digits: 3) m, time of flight #calc.round(2 * 10 * calc.sin(20deg) / g, digits: 3) s.
]
